---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Everdell Card Types Explained: All Five Colours"
  description="Travelers, Production, Destination, Governance and Prosperity — what each colour is actually for."
  game="everdell"
  gameTitle="Everdell"
  category="Rules"
  product="everdell-open-signs"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Everdell's cards come in five colours, and the colour tells you
    <em>when the card does its thing</em>. Once that clicks, reading a new card
    takes a second rather than a minute — and building a balanced city stops being
    guesswork.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Tan acts once when played, green acts every season, red is a location others can visit, blue acts when triggered by other cards, and purple only scores at the end." />

  <ComparisonTable title="The five colours at a glance">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Colour</th>
          <th>Type</th>
          <th>When it acts</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>🟤 Tan</td>
          <td>Traveler</td>
          <td>Immediately, once, when played</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>🟢 Green</td>
          <td>Production</td>
          <td>When played, and every time you prepare for a season</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>🔴 Red</td>
          <td>Destination</td>
          <td>When a worker is placed on it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>🔵 Blue</td>
          <td>Governance</td>
          <td>Whenever its trigger condition happens</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>🟣 Purple</td>
          <td>Prosperity</td>
          <td>Only at the very end, as scoring</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>🟤 Travelers (Tan)</h2>

  <p>
    Tan cards fire once, the moment you play them, and then sit in your city doing
    nothing but taking up one of your fifteen slots.
  </p>

  <p>
    That sounds bad, and for weak Travelers it is. But the strong ones give you a
    large one-off burst exactly when you need it — a pile of resources, a fistful
    of cards — and in a game this tight, tempo has real value. Judge a Traveler by
    whether its immediate effect is worth a permanent slot.
  </p>

  <h2>🟢 Production (Green)</h2>

  <p>
    Green cards produce resources when you play them, and again every time you
    prepare for a new season. They are the engine of Everdell.
  </p>

  <p>
    The timing is what matters: a green card played in Winter fires on entering
    Spring, Summer <em>and</em> Autumn — three payouts. The identical card played
    in Autumn fires once and never again.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Green cards are worth more the earlier you play them">
    This single fact drives Everdell's opening. Winter and early Spring should be
    spent building production, even when the cards themselves score few points.
    Points bought in Autumn come from resources your engine generated.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>🔴 Destinations (Red)</h2>

  <p>
    Red cards create a worker space inside your own city. You can place a worker
    there to get its benefit — and depending on the card,
    <strong>other players may be able to visit it too</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Red cards are effectively extra actions, which is enormous in a game where
    you only have a handful of workers. The catch is that a shared Destination
    can be occupied by an opponent before you get to it, and open Destinations
    are easy to lose track of across four cities.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Check whether a Destination is open or closed">
    Some red cards are yours alone; others are open to the whole table. Getting
    this wrong is one of the most common Everdell rules errors, and it usually
    isn't spotted until someone's worker is already sitting there.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>🔵 Governance (Blue)</h2>

  <p>
    Blue cards do nothing on their own. Each one waits for a condition — you play
    a Construction, you play a Critter, you gain a particular resource — and then
    quietly gives you something.
  </p>

  <p>
    Blue is the colour beginners underrate most. A blue card that discounts every
    future Construction, in a city where you're going to play eight more
    Constructions, is worth more than almost anything else you could put in that
    slot. Blue cards get better the earlier they arrive and the more cards you
    play after them.
  </p>

  <h2>🟣 Prosperity (Purple)</h2>

  <p>
    Purple cards do absolutely nothing during play. At the end of the game each
    one scores based on what your finished city contains — how many of a certain
    colour you built, how many Critters, and so on.
  </p>

  <p>
    They're expensive, they're pure points, and they belong in Autumn. Playing a
    purple card early costs you an engine slot for a whole game and gains you
    nothing until the final count.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="The natural shape of a good city">
    Green and blue early, red in the middle to buy extra actions, tan
    opportunistically when the burst is worth the slot, purple last. If your
    city is following roughly that arc, you're playing Everdell well.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Constructions vs Critters</h2>

  <p>
    Colour is only half the picture. Every card is also either a
    <strong>Construction</strong> or a <strong>Critter</strong>, and many
    Constructions let you play a specific named Critter for free.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those pairs are where Everdell's value is concentrated. Playing a Farm and
    then a free Husband is two cards for the price of one — and in a game where
    resources are the binding constraint, "free" is the strongest word on any
    card.
  </p>

  <p>
    How to spend your fifteen slots across all this is covered in
    <a href="/games/everdell/everdell-beginner-strategy/">building a city that scores</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
